title: Value types
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: reference
summary: The ten value types, what each holds, and their JSON form.

|!Type |!Holds |!JSON `value` |
|`nothing` |an explicit absence: "this field does not apply" |none (`{"type": "nothing"}`) |
|`string` |UTF-8 text |a string |
|`int` |a 64-bit signed integer |a number |
|`float` |a 64-bit float |a number |
|`bool` |true or false |a boolean |
|`datetime` |an instant, stored as Unix milliseconds (UTC) |an ISO-8601 string, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ` |
|`ref` |another metarecord of the same repository |its uuid |
|`tree_ref` |a position in a named tree: a parent and a name |`{"parent": <uuid or null>, "name": …}` |
|`refbase` |a repository |its uuid |
|`externalref` |a metarecord of another repository |`{"repo": <uuid>, "metarecord": <uuid>}` |

Every value travels as `{"type": "<type>", "value": …}`, uuids as 32 lowercase hex
characters without hyphens (the hyphenated form is accepted on input).

A `tree_ref`'s parent is `null` for a root. Several independent trees coexist in one
repository under different field names — `mfr_path` for the filesystem, `path` for
the tag hierarchy — each a forest of its own. A name has a displayed form and its
exact bytes, which identify the node (a file name need not be valid UTF-8).

The four reference types are ''distinct'' types, not one family: a field is `ref`,
or `refbase`, or `externalref`, or `tree_ref`, never a mix. A link that is sometimes
local and sometimes cross-repository is modelled as `externalref` throughout.

On the command line a value is written as a field specification, `name:type=value`
([[Field specifications]]).
